      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-10 mb-4">How to Apply for a Provincial Nomination</h2>

      <p>The application process varies by province and stream but generally follows these steps: First, create an Express Entry profile with IRCC to establish your presence in the pool. Second, research which provinces have streams that match your occupation, qualifications, and connections. Third, submit an application to the provincial program, following their specific requirements for documentation, fees, and timelines. Fourth, if the province issues you a nomination, you will receive a notification in your Express Entry account and the 600 CRS points will be added to your score. Fifth, you will then receive an ITA in the next Express Entry draw and can proceed with your permanent residence application.</p>

      <p>Some provinces, particularly Ontario and Nova Scotia, use a passive invitation model where they search the Express Entry pool and issue Notifications of Interest to candidates who meet their criteria. In these cases, you do not need to apply proactively - the province identifies you based on your profile. Other provinces, such as British Columbia and Saskatchewan, use an active application model where you submit an expression of interest or application directly to the provincial program.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-10 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">How many CRS points does a provincial nomination add?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">A provincial or territorial nomination adds 600 CRS points to your Express Entry profile. Since most Express Entry draws have cutoff scores in the 400-530 range, the additional 600 points virtually guarantees that you will receive an Invitation to Apply in the next draw after your nomination is accepted.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">Can I apply to multiple Provincial Nominee Programs at the same time?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Yes, you can apply to PNPs in multiple provinces simultaneously, as each province operates its own independent program. However, you can only accept one nomination in your Express Entry profile. If you receive nominations from multiple provinces, you must choose which one to accept. Some provinces may have policies discouraging multiple simultaneous applications.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">Do I have to live in the province that nominates me?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">While there is no legal requirement under federal law to live in the nominating province permanently, you are expected to demonstrate genuine intent to settle there. Provinces monitor nominees and may withdraw or not renew nominations if they believe the applicant has no genuine intention to live and work in the province. Moving to a different province immediately after obtaining PR could jeopardize future applicants from that program.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

